  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vanuatu - Earthquake</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Phase: Rapid damage assessments, distribution of emergency shelter and household items, health services, WASH support, and cash assistance.
Recovery Phase: Rehabilitating damaged infrastructure, restoring livelihoods through cash support and in-kind aid, providing mental health and psychosocial support, and conducting disaster risk reduction initiatives.
Coordination: Utilizing the IFRC&apos;s Federation-wide approach, involving VRCS, the IFRC, and partner National Societies for a coordinated response.
Funding: A total funding requirement of CHF 5.2 million, with CHF 5 million allocated for the IFRC and CHF 0.2 million from other Movement partners.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The earthquake caused widespread destruction, severely damaging infrastructure, disrupting essential services (healthcare, water, electricity), and displacing thousands. Vulnerable populations, including women, children, the elderly, and people with disabilities, face heightened risks. Immediate and long-term humanitarian support is necessary to save lives, restore basic services, and rebuild community resilience.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operation targets 50,000 people, specifically:

Families who lost loved ones or whose members were severely injured.
Households with damaged or destroyed homes.
Vulnerable groups: people with disabilities, elderly, pregnant/lactating women, single female-headed households, and children under five.
Displaced families and those suffering from income loss due to destroyed businesses.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operational strategy aims to respond to the humanitarian needs following a 7.3 magnitude earthquake that struck Vanuatu on 17 December 2024. The plan focuses on providing emergency relief and supporting early recovery for 50,000 affected people. Key areas of intervention include shelter, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), health, livelihoods recovery, and disaster risk reduction.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The response is focused on Efate Island and five nearby islands in Shefa Province, where the majority of the damage occurred, especially in and around Port Vila, Vanuatu&apos;s capital.</narrative>
  </description>
